    {
      "id": "integration",
      "name": "Integration",
      "description": "It does not choose its own places; it grows out of the ones the horse already has. Anywhere the coat is genuinely dark - a bay's black points, the crest and the base of the mane, a dorsal stripe, the margin of any other dark marking - becomes a source, and the field propagates outward from it. Right at a source the elements merge into continuous patches with scalloped margins; a hand's span out they are separate circles and lobes; a foot or two out they thin to nothing, and beyond that there is none. A horse with no dark on it anywhere shows almost none of this, and the same horse with black points is covered from the knees up.",
      "when": [
        "Int/Int",
        "Int/n",
        "Int/Inc"
      ],
